The conversation in response to this posting has
gone far off track. I think it's time we get back to
your original questions.

Yes, it makes no sense that a tool that can chop out
an entire level, through solid rock that when
excavated by nonPC dwarves leaves huge boulders, and
can dig pits on every bit of it, without losing its
edge, becomes dull when used to chip a handfull of
letters into that very same level's very same floor.

Yes, the engraving might as well be into the rock;
the tools are rock-capable, they shouldn't be
limited to dust.

Yes, by the time you can find an unfortunate dwarf's
pickaxe or dwarven mattock, or a random broad pick,
you're probably carrying far more gems than you'd
prefer to tote, and one or more of them is most
likely a hard gem.

_However_, just from a comparision with weilding a
knife or dagger, no, it doesn't make a bit of sense
that a pickaxe or mattock could engrave more than a
single letter per turn. They were never designed for
"fine work", and would be awkward as heck to use for
scribing entire verses.

I think adding "undullable when engraving" but also
adding "slow as mud when engraving" would keep game
balance fairly well, and the ultimate engraving
tools of choice would still be athames, wands of
fire, or wands of digging, so mass-engraver
wannabies wouldn't stop looking after acquiring one
of the pick/mattock class of digging tools.

xanthian.

Which reminds me of a YANI: wouldn't it be splendid,
in tile form or glyph form, if floor/corridor cells
containing engravings were somehow made obvious from
afar?  In that Real World which NetHack Is Not, it
is often fairly easy to spot text somehow included
in the walking surface from quite a distance away,
at least in lighted areas.



